<!--
  The typed coordination engine, as a `dotnet tool` (ADR-0034 Phase 3a).

  THE FLIP HAS SINCE HAPPENED (ADR-0040 Phase D COMPLETE). This tool is no longer packaged to *shadow*
  bash — it IS the coordination client. `scripts/fsgg-coord` is now the §4.4 shim that execs this
  command, the ~7,000-line bash monolith is deleted, and the shadow, the `engine=` mode flag, and the
  fleet-divergence ledger are retired. The history below is why it was packaged BEFORE the flip; it is
  kept as the record of that ordering.

  WHY IT WAS PACKAGED FIRST, AND WHY THAT WAS *NOT* THE FLIP.

  Phase 2 shipped the shadow: both engines decide, bash's answer is returned, the disagreement is
  logged, and bash stays authoritative until that log is clean. Phase 2's exit criterion is "zero
  divergence across the LIVE FLEET for three consecutive days" — and it could never be met, because
  the engine existed only where somebody had BUILT it, which is `.github` and nowhere else. Fleet
  evidence needs the engine distributed; distribution was scheduled for Phase 3; Phase 3 was gated on
  Phase 2 exiting. The clock could never start (.github#617).

  So publishing came BEFORE flipping, and it was the only order in which the flip's evidence could
  exist. Bash stayed authoritative through Phase 3a. The single thing packaging changed was that a
  worker in a receiver repo now HAD an engine to shadow with.

  IDENTITY IS FOREVER (ADR-0003; "package identity == product identity, never rename in place"):

    PackageId        FS.GG.Coord.Cli     — the name ADR-0034 §4.1 gives it, and the right one for what
                                           it BECOMES: at the flip this is the coordination client.
    ToolCommandName  fsgg-coord-engine   — and it never changes either. At the flip, the `kit:` row
                                           `scripts/fsgg-coord` becomes a shim that EXECS this command,
                                           so the command name never has to be renamed and no tool
                                           manifest ever has to be rewritten.

  STABLE, NOT -preview. The org promoted its last `-preview` producer in July, and the shared Renovate
  preset now pins to stable (`ignoreUnstable: true`). A `-preview` tool would be INVISIBLE to Renovate
  — and Renovate-invisibility is one of the stated reasons ADR-0034 §4.4 rejected the self-contained
  binary. Shipping a tool the update fabric cannot see would defeat the point of shipping it.

  `.github` builds this FROM SOURCE and never restores it from the feed. That asymmetry is what breaks
  the publish cycle: a broken feed cannot stop the coordination tool being fixed, because the repo that
  fixes it does not depend on the feed. Only RECEIVERS consume the package.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RollForward>Major</RollForward>

    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>fsgg-coord-engine</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>FS.GG.Coord.Cli</PackageId>
    <!-- 0.14.0 to 0.15.0 is a MINOR, and under 0.x SemVer that is also the BREAKING signal — the rule
         registry/dependencies.yml's coord-engine row states three times: "on the 0.x line an observable-
         to-callers behaviour change is spelled a minor, because 0.x has no major to take" (at 0.13.0 and
         restated at 0.14.0), and "a 0.x minor is the only spelling available for it" (at 0.12.0). Patch
         is not defensible here, and MAJOR IS NOT AVAILABLE — that is the whole reason this line reads the
         way it does. TEN engine commits ship (.github#1906), and SIX force the minor independently:

           * #1680 (06c1887) — THE WIRE SURFACE. `landable` returns exit **10** (`merged` / `closed`), a
             code it could not return before, and prints two verdict words it could not print before. A
             caller that switched exhaustively on the old five codes meets an unrecognised status. This
             one alone forces the minor, and it is the reason this cut is OWED rather than merely due:
             `check-engine-freshness.py` reds on `src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/Protocol.fs` and has been red on
             main since that merge.
           * #1740 (474ef9e), #1779 (071780e), #1792 (bfcdefb) — the #353 collision gate behind `widen`,
             `set-paths` and `overlap` in its active mode no longer decides reservation from the board's `Status`
             COLUMN, and no longer asks the identity function a reservation question. It answers OVERLAP
             where 0.14.0 answered DISJOINT — so a `widen` 0.14.0 PERMITS now refuses. Measured live on
             2026-07-28: DISJOINT (wrong) -> OVERLAP (right) over one unchanged board.
           * #1794 (06b8ac7) — `Reads.openIssues` FAILS CLOSED. An unidentifiable element or an unreadable
             body over a live reservation now refuses the whole read rather than silently answering
             "declares nothing". Calls that exited 0 on a partially-unreadable board now exit non-zero.
           * #1668 (c6489d1) — `who` gains a FOURTH row state, `UNDETERMINED`, with its own `.state` word
             in the json projection. Any consumer switching exhaustively on that vocabulary meets a new value; the
             residue is filed as #1897.
           * #1677 (fc1a8aa) — `FSGG_CLAIM_LEASE_MIN` is READ. It was named across the repo and honoured
             by no line of code, so a worker who exported it now gets a DIFFERENT lease than 0.14.0 gave
             them, and a malformed value now exits 1 where 0.14.0 silently used 120.
           * #1823 (b3f0e2e) — `add` WRITES `Status: Backlog` where 0.14.0 left the column unset. A verb
             that made one board write now makes two, and the filed row is schedulable where it was not.

         #1666 (7019939) additionally changes what `budget` reports and how a 403 is classified (secondary
         limits are told from the primary budget and carry no reset). #1654 (5fbde62) is a comment-only
         correction in the `command-contract` emitter and moves no behaviour.

         This property is the RELEASE ACT (see scripts/check-engine-freshness.py): the engine's version
         moves only at release time, by hand, and release-coord-engine.yml evaluates it with
         `dotnet msbuild -getProperty:Version` and refuses any `coord-engine/v[version]` tag that
         disagrees. The tag is a coherence check against this line and never its source. -->
    <Version>0.15.0</Version>
    <AssemblyName>fsgg-coord-engine</AssemblyName>

    <Description>FS-GG typed coordination engine — the schedulability model behind `fsgg-coord` as one total function over a board-state snapshot, plus the typed IO layer that drives it. It IS the coordination tool (ADR-0034/ADR-0040): the `fsgg-coord` shim resolves and execs it; there is no bash left to shadow.</Description>
    <Authors>FS-GG</Authors>
    <Company>FS-GG</Company>
    <PackageTags>fsgg;coordination;scheduler;dotnet-tool;fsharp</PackageTags>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github/tree/main/src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli</PackageProjectUrl>

    <!-- THE RELEASE NOTES ARE WHERE THE BREAK IS ANNOUNCED, and that is the point of them here.
         nuget.org renders this on the package listing and `dotnet tool` consumers read it there. A
         changelog that said only "fixes" while a previously-accepted flag started exiting 1 would be
         the #266 shape in its purest form: the org knowing a thing, and the consumer discovering it
         from a broken script. Criterion 5 of .github#1596 asks for exactly this.

         KEEP THIS PROPERTY IN STEP WITH <Version> ABOVE. The 0.13.0 cut moved <Version> and left these
         notes reading "0.12.0 — BREAKING...", so the nuget.org listing for 0.13.0 announced the PREVIOUS
         release's break and none of its own seven commits. Nothing gates the two against each other
         (.github#1762). A consumer who reads the listing and finds the wrong release described has been
         failed by it exactly as much as one who finds nothing. -->
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.15.0 — MINOR on the 0.x line, which is also the 0.x BREAKING signal.

TEN engine commits since 0.14.0, and ONE OF THEM MOVES THE WIRE SURFACE
(src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/Protocol.fs). That file is the source the exit-code tables in every worker-facing
skill are GENERATED from, so between .github#1680 landing and this release the org's own documents
described a verb contract no published engine implemented. This release is what makes them true again.

THE REASON TO TAKE THIS RELEASE: every worker vendors `scripts/fsgg-coord`, which resolves and execs
this package. A receiver on 0.14.0 runs a collision gate that answers DISJOINT over live locks, a
`landable` that burns 600 seconds waiting on already-merged work, and an `add` that files rows no
scheduler can see.

BREAKING — THE WIRE SURFACE. `landable` answers a PR THAT IS NOT OPEN with exit 10 (.github#1680).
GitHub reports mergeable as null on a merged PR, which the four-row match read as "still computing" and
returned as `pending` / exit 7 — the ONE code the contract defines as worth retrying, for the most
terminal state there is. So the waiting form spent its entire 600s budget (30 tries x 20s) on a settled
fact, every time, and the caller that meets this most is the RECOVERY path, which re-gates a PR whose
worker died between merge and stamp.

  $ fsgg-coord-engine landable 1675 --wait
      0.14.0:  pending   rc=7    after 600s
      0.15.0:  merged    rc=10   after 0.69s

Exit 10 prints one of two words on stdout and they call for OPPOSITE acts. `merged` means the work
LANDED: do not merge it again, go STAMP it. `closed` means nothing landed: do NOT stamp it done. It is
deliberately not 3 (red/conflicted), because folding a success into the stop code would tell that
successor to give up rather than to finish.

WHO THIS BREAKS: any caller that switched exhaustively over `landable`'s previous five codes, or that
treated "not 0 and not 7" as a failure. Exit 10 on `merged` is a SUCCESS. An unmet `--sha` on the merged
arm is now reported with its own sentences rather than dropped.

BREAKING — THE #353 COLLISION GATE REFUSES WHERE IT USED TO PERMIT. Three fixes, one surface:
`widen`, `set-paths` and `overlap --active`.

  * .github#1740 — the scan was served from the 90-second scheduling cache, and a `Status` write can sit
    QUEUED on an exhausted budget, so the freshest possible board still showed the pre-claim column.
  * .github#1779 — the candidate set is no longer derived from the board at all. `claim` exits GREEN with
    that column unwritten in FOUR ways and the cache/queue fixes could reach only two. The scan now keys
    on the LOCK: `Reads.openIssues` lists open issues with their bodies in one paginated call, token
    conflicts are computed on those free bodies, and a marker is read only for a row that actually
    collides. Measured either side of one `overlap --active` on the live board: 24/27/31 GraphQL points
    and the wrong answer, to 0/0/0 points and the right one.
  * .github#1792 — `Scan.snapshot` asked `reserver` (a lapsed lease still reserves) while the collision
    gate asked `winner` (it does not), so "is this file reserved?" had two answers depending on which
    verb asked. The gate asks a reservation question and now calls the reservation function.

  $ fsgg-coord-engine widen .github#123 --paths src/A.fs
      0.14.0:  DISJOINT   (over a live, uncolumned claim on src/A.fs)
      0.15.0:  OVERLAP

WHO THIS BREAKS: anything that read DISJOINT as licence to edit. There is no CAS on a file, so nothing
downstream ever re-decided that answer — which is why a wrong DISJOINT was final and this is the fix
worth taking the release for. Two workers really did edit one file this way on 2026-07-28.

BREAKING — READS FAIL CLOSED (.github#1794). `Reads.openIssues` DROPPED an element with no numeric
`number` silently, and read an absent or ill-typed `body` as an empty string — which parses as
"declares nothing" and collides with nothing. Both failed OPEN, into the gate above, where a wrong
DISJOINT is final. Now an unidentifiable element refuses the whole read quoting `element N of M`, and an
unreadable body is carried to the marker phase: no reserver means it is safely skipped, a reserver means
we may not answer DISJOINT over a live reservation, so it refuses and names the row and the holder.
`body: null` stays a real observed empty body. Calls that exited 0 over a partially unreadable board now
exit non-zero — that is the point of the change, not a regression.

BREAKING — `who` GAINS A FOURTH ROW STATE (.github#1668). `Reads.markers` classified every comment as
marker-or-nothing, so two failures to READ were indistinguishable from a successful observation of
ABSENCE, and `Ok []` on an In-progress row rendered as `UNCLAIMED` with the "someone is working outside
the protocol" accusation attached. That was printed 32 seconds before a `--force` deleted a live marker
on the same item. `who` now has `UNDETERMINED`, with a distinct `.state` word in `--json`, no
accusation, and an explicit instruction not to dispatch, `reap` or `adopt`. The incompleteness rides
EVERY row, not only markerless ones: a hidden marker with a lower id makes a `held` row name the wrong
holder. Consumers switching exhaustively over the state vocabulary meet a new value (residue: #1897).

BREAKING — `FSGG_CLAIM_LEASE_MIN` IS ACTUALLY READ (.github#1677). It was named across this repo as the
way to configure the claim lease and honoured by no line of code; `--lease` was the only control that
ever existed. Precedence is now `--lease` then `FSGG_CLAIM_LEASE_MIN` then 120. A malformed value is
REFUSED (exit 1, naming the variable and what was read), identically to `--lease abc` — deliberately,
because falling back to 120 on garbage would rebuild the exact unfalsifiability this fixes. Unset and
empty mean "not configured" and take the default.

WHO THIS BREAKS: anyone who exported the variable and got 120 anyway. They now get the lease they asked
for, which may not be the lease their scripts were tuned against.

BREAKING for board writers — `add` DEFAULTS `Status` TO `Backlog` (.github#1823). A row with no `Status`
is invisible to every scheduler. Fourteen were filed that way on 2026-07-28, in three batches, and every
one was found by accident. `Backlog` and not `Ready`: visible to triage, not startable without a
deliberate promotion. THE DEFAULT ONLY EVER FILLS AN EMPTY COLUMN — `add` is idempotent and re-run
routinely, so the already-on-board arm reads the column first and an unreadable column is left alone
rather than defaulted. `add` never becomes a refusal: an underivable identity degrades the column by
name instead of refusing the filing. Ships with the `STATUS-UNSET` chore, which reports rather than
prevents.

CHANGED DIAGNOSTICS — A SECONDARY RATE LIMIT IS TOLD FROM THE PRIMARY BUDGET (.github#1666). `EX_RATE`
halted a six-worker fan-out repeatedly while the primary REST budget read 62%, 87%, 88% free, and a
retry seconds later succeeded. Four diagnoses were filed on that reading; three were withdrawn by their
own authors. There was never an internal counter — `Budget.classify` reacts to a real 403, computed
which of five wordings matched, discarded that, and rendered ONE sentence for THREE mechanisms. A
secondary (abuse-detection) limit is triggered by burst CONCURRENCY, fires with the primary counter
untouched, and NEVER APPEARS IN /rate_limit; the reset was being read off the primary window, which is
why "resets in ~6m" preceded a retry that succeeded in seconds; and `Retry-After`, the only header that
describes a secondary limit, was read nowhere. `RateLimitResource` now carries the mechanism and the
bucket, `SecondaryLimit` gets no resetAt, and the remedy is stated correctly: reduce concurrency, do not
wait fleet-wide. An unclassifiable 403 fails closed. `budget` now says whose budget it reports and what
it omits. The same defect was found and fixed in three more sites (Board, Scan, Done), which classified
GraphQL error arrays with `isRateLimited` and advised "REST-only work may still run" into the detector
that had just fired.

ALSO: a comment-only correction in the `command-contract` emitter (.github#1654) — it argued against a
schema bump using an asymmetry .github#1574 had already removed. No behaviour moves.</PackageReleaseNotes>

    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!-- RestorePackagesWithLockFile, RestoreLockedMode and DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder (ADR-0006,
         ADR-0032) are INHERITED from the root Directory.Build.props, which imports the org-shared
         config this repo authors and ships. Every project here used to set them by hand because
         .github did not consume its own build config (#609). It does now. -->

    <!-- The engine is invoked once per scheduling call, on every worker, in a fan-out. Startup cost
         is the whole of its runtime cost, so it takes no dependency it does not need. No
         Spectre.Console here: JSON is the contract, and the text projection is a handful of printfn. -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Json.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Json.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Snapshot.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Snapshot.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Options.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Options.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Identity.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Identity.fs" />
    <Compile Include="FollowupAudit.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="FollowupAudit.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Chores.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Chores.fs" />
    <Compile Include="KitDigest.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="KitDigest.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Render.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Render.fs" />
    <Compile Include="ReadyApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="ReadyApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="LintApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="LintApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Client.fs" />
    <!-- AFTER `Client.fs`, deliberately: the follow-up queue reuses `parseRefIn` and the exit constants
         rather than growing a second answer to "what is a ref?" or "what does an empty look exit?" (#485).
         It is a LOCAL verb — no token, no board — and depends on `Client` for those two things only. -->
    <Compile Include="Followups.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Followups.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../FS.GG.Coord.Core/FS.GG.Coord.Core.fsproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
